4. AUTOMATED CLA BOT (GITHUB DEPLOYMENT
DESIGN)
This is the production system that enforces everything above.
You typically implement this as a GitHub App + PR status gate.
4.1 System Architecture
Components:
1. GitHub App (CLA Bot)
2. Webhook listener (Node.js / Python / Go)
3. Signature database (PostgreSQL / DynamoDB)
4. PR status check API
5. Optional: signature verification (email / SSO / signed token)
4.2 Workflow (PR Gate Enforcement)
Trigger:
Pull Request opened or updated
Step 1 — CLA Check
Bot checks:
Has contributor signed CLA?
Is signature valid and stored?
If NO:
Post comment:
Add failing status check: cla-check: failed
Step 2 — AI Attestation Check
Bot verifies PR template contains CAS section:
If missing:
request update
block merge
Step 3 — Signature Capture Flow (if missing CLA)
Bot posts:
“Please accept the Contributor License Agreement to proceed.”
Provides link:
User signs → stored in DB:
{
 "github_user": "username",
 "email": "user@email.com",
 "cla_version": "2.0",
 "timestamp": "2026-04-30",
 "ai_attestation": true

Step 4 — Approval Gate
If:
CLA signed ✔
AI attestation present ✔
no policy violations ✔
Then bot sets:
cla-check: passed
Allow merge.
